; bridge_uart_baudot.loom
; A UART-to-teleprinter bridge: thread 0 receives ASCII on a serial line
; (8N1, for example 115200 baud from a PC), translates it into
; International Telegraph Alphabet No. 2 (ITA2, ITU-T S.1) and inserts the
; letter-shift and figure-shift combinations the text needs; thread 1
; sends the codes as 7.5-unit start-stop characters (a 1.5-unit stop
; element) at teleprinter speed. No host in the loop: the host loads the
; image, sets the two ticks and starts both threads.
;
; Written on 2026-11-07, after the hardware was complete (D-032), as a
; protocol Loom was not designed for (D-035). It uses the M2 core (pins,
; deadlines, the deadline-latched pin write of 6.10 for every teleprinter
; edge), thread 0's bit engine in manual mode (SHI shifts the UART bits
; in, 6.9, built for the synchronous serial programs) and LD/ST to data
; memory (6.11, built so that device emulators can keep a register file)
; for the translation table and the queue between the two threads. No
; hardware knows ITA2, its shift state, a five-unit code or a half-unit
; stop element: all of it is firmware. Thread 0's receiver is
; firmware/bridge_uart_dmx.loom's; thread 1's transmitter is
; firmware/baudot_tx.loom with its INQ replaced by the queue.
;
; Pins: RX = IN0 (thread 0, the UART input), TX = OUT0 (thread 1, the
; teleprinter loop: 1 = condition Z, stop polarity, mark; S.1 3.2), and
; CTS_N = OUT1, a flow-control output for the UART sender, low = clear to
; send (the sense of an FTDI or CP210x CTS# input). The UART side is about
; 2000 times faster than the teleprinter, so a sender that ignores CTS_N
; overruns the queue after 63 codes and the bridge drops what does not fit.
;
; --------------------------------------------------------- the translation
; One table word per ASCII code 0x00 .. 0x7F (TABLE below): bits 4:0 the
; ITA2 combination (code element 1 in bit 0, S.1 Table 1), bits 6:5 its
; case: 1 letters, 2 figures, 3 either (space, carriage return, line feed:
; S.1 4.4 makes only combinations 1 .. 26 depend on the shift), 0 none.
; Lower case is sent as upper case (S.1 2.3: ITA2 has one series of
; letters). The figure case carries - ? : 3 8 ( ) . , 9 0 1 4 ' 5 7 = 2 /
; 6 + (S.1 2.1, Table 1) and BEL is the audible signal, figure J (4.3).
; Every other byte is DROPPED, not replaced: ! " # $ % & * ; < > @ [ \ ] ^
; _ ` { | } ~, DEL, the other control codes and bytes 0x80 .. 0xFF, and
; also ENQ (it would be "who are you?", figure D, which starts the far
; end's answerback: S.1 4.1; see QUESTIONS) and NUL (all-space, combination
; 32, must not be sent during a call: S.1 4.7.2). F, G and H in figure
; case are not defined internationally (S.1 4.2) and are never sent.
;
; The shift rules. The case the far end is in is tracked in r3: unknown at
; start, so the first letter, figure or either-case character is preceded
; by a shift (S.1 4.4: the first combination transmitted shall be a
; shift). A letter in figure case (or unknown) gets LTRS first, a figure in
; letter case gets FIGS first. One more rule, for receivers with "unshift
; on space" (a common amateur-radio setting that S.1 does not have): after
; a space sent in figure case the case is taken as unknown, so the next
; letter or figure gets its shift again. An S.1 receiver ignores the
; repeated shift (it is already in that case) and an unshift-on-space
; receiver needs it, so the output prints the same on both.
;
; UART rate: 8 ticks a bit. The host sets thread 0's TICK_INT/TICK_FRAC
; to one eighth of a bit before RUN (115200 baud: 54 + 64/256; the RTL
; tests: 32, 256 clocks a bit). The deadline pairs are proved for ticks of
; 32 clocks or more (.tick below); samples sit within -P+12 .. +12 clocks
; of mid-bit (P the tick). A character is translated during its own stop
; bit; after the stop sample the queue write takes at most 22 slots (88
; clocks), inside the half bit (4P) before a back-to-back start bit.
;
; Teleprinter rate: 32 ticks a unit, thread 1's TICK_INT as for
; firmware/baudot_tx.loom (45.45 bauds: 34375; 50 bauds: 31250; 75 bauds:
; 20833 + 85/256; the RTL tests: 16, 512 clocks a unit). Every edge is
; latched (6.10), every element exactly 32 ticks and the stop element 48
; when codes follow back to back, as in baudot_tx.loom (R.103 sections 1,
; 2.2; at least the 1.25-unit stop of 3.3 always). Proved for ticks of 16
; clocks or more.
;
; ------------------------------------------------------------- the queue
; A ring of 64 codes at RING .. RING + 63, with two shared words, each
; written by one thread only: HEAD (thread 0: the next entry it writes)
; and TAIL (thread 1: the next entry it reads). Thread 0 writes an entry
; and only then publishes HEAD; thread 1 reads an entry and only then
; publishes TAIL; an LD or ST is one access at one edge (6.11), so neither
; ever sees a half-written word, and with at most 63 entries in use the
; writer never overtakes the reader. A character takes one or two entries
; (its shift and itself) and goes in whole or not at all.
;
; Flow control on CTS_N. Thread 0 sets CTS_N = 1 (stop) after a write
; that leaves fewer than LOW free entries; thread 1 sets CTS_N = 0 (go)
; after a read that leaves HIGH or more. Two threads write this one output
; pin, which is safe here: each thread writes only its own value and the
; pin holds the last write (it is not a wired-AND, COMMON.md), and the free
; count cannot move from under LOW to HIGH or back in the few slots
; between a thread's read of the other's index and its pin write (a UART
; character adds at most 2 entries in 10 bits, a teleprinter character
; removes 1 in 7.5 units). A sender that stops within LOW / 2 = 8
; characters of CTS_N rising loses nothing (FTDI parts stop within 3).
;
; Memory, all 512 words: thread 0's code 84 words (0 .. 83), thread 1's
; 45 words (128 .. 172), 259 words loaded in all,
; the table at 256 .. 383 (thread 2's quarter), HEAD and TAIL at 384, 385,
; the ring at 386 .. 449 (thread 3's quarter). Threads 2 and 3 cannot run
; beside it.
;
; Readings taken on the open spec questions (2026-11-07):
;   Unshift on space: kept. The shift is re-sent after a space sent in
;   figure case, so S.1 receivers and "unshift on space" receivers print
;   the same; it costs one combination and S.1 4.4 allows it.
;   A shift after a long idle: not sent. S.1 4.4 asks for one when
;   transmission commences, which the first character after start gets;
;   doing it after idle would need a third shared word.
;   ENQ: dropped, not mapped to WRU, so a stray byte cannot make the far
;   end send its answerback.
;   Figure case: the international ITA2 table only; no US TTY variant.

        .imem   512                     ; the macro build: quarters of 128 words
        .pins   RX = IN0, TX = OUT0, CTS_N = OUT1
        .equ    OVS = 8                 ; UART ticks a bit
        .equ    TPB = 32                ; teleprinter ticks a unit
        .equ    STOP = TPB + TPB / 2    ; the 1.5-unit stop element
        .equ    LEAD = 4                ; thread 1 takes the next code this early
        .equ    TABLE = 256             ; 128 words, one per ASCII code
        .equ    SHARED = 384            ; HEAD, TAIL
        .equ    RING = SHARED + 2       ; 64 entries
        .equ    MASK = 63               ; ring index mask
        .equ    LOW = 16                ; free entries under which CTS_N stops the sender
        .equ    HIGH = 32               ; free entries from which CTS_N lets it go
        .equ    LET = 1                 ; the cases (table bits 6:5, and r3)
        .equ    FIG = 2
        .equ    EITHER = 3
        .equ    LTRS = 0x1F             ; S.1 Table 1, combination 29
        .equ    FIGS = 0x1B             ; combination 30
        .equ    SPACE = 0x04            ; combination 31

; ============================================== thread 0: UART to the ring
; r0 the byte, then its combination; r1 SHARED; r2 the free entries; r3 the
; case the far end is in (0 unknown, LET, FIG); r4 HEAD; r5 the combination
; to write, 0 for none; r6 the case after it; r7 the shift to write first,
; 0 for none (combination 32 is never sent, so 0 is free to mean that).
        .thread 0
        .tick   32                      ; the shortest tick the pairs are proved for
        .csr    BE_PINS, pin(RX) << 5   ; SHI reads RX; BE_CFG 0: LSB first
        LDI     r3, 0                   ; the case: unknown (S.1 4.4)
        LDI     r4, 0                   ; HEAD = TAIL = 0 at load

rx:     WAITP   RX, 0                   ; the start bit (or already low)
        SETD    0                       ; re-anchor on its edge
        WAITD   OVS / 2                 ; the middle of the start bit
        JP      RX, 1, rx               ; high again: a glitch
        LDI     r0, 8
        CSRW    CNT, r0                 ; eight SHIs
bit:    WAITD   OVS                     ; the middle of the next data bit
        SHI                             ; sample; Z on the eighth
        BNZ     bit
        STSR    r0
        SHRI    r0, 8                   ; the byte, from SR 15:8

; Translate during the stop bit: r5, r7, r6 and the room are decided here.
        LDI     r5, 0                   ; nothing to write unless found below
        MOV     r7, r0
        SHRI    r7, 7                   ; Z: 0x00 .. 0x7F
        BNZ     stop                    ; 0x80 .. 0xFF: no ITA2 code, dropped
        MOV16   r7, TABLE
        ADD     r7, r7, r0
        LD      r0, r7, 0               ; the table word
        MOV     r6, r0
        SHRI    r6, 5                   ; r6 = its case; Z: no code, dropped
        BZ      stop
        ANDI    r0, 31                  ; r0 = the combination
        LDI     r7, 0                   ; no shift, unless one is needed
        CMPI    r6, EITHER
        BZ      either
        CMP     r6, r3                  ; a letter or a figure: in that case?
        BZ      room
        LDI     r7, LTRS                ; no: its shift first
        CMPI    r6, LET
        BZ      room
        LDI     r7, FIGS
        JMP     room
either: MOV     r6, r3                  ; space, CR, LF: the case stays...
        TEST    r3, r3
        BNZ     usos
        LDI     r7, LTRS                ; ...but nothing has been sent yet: a
        LDI     r6, LET                 ; shift first (S.1 4.4)
        JMP     room
usos:   CMPI    r0, SPACE               ; a space in figure case: the case is
        BNZ     room                    ; then unknown (unshift-on-space
        CMPI    r3, FIG                 ; receivers are back in letters)
        BNZ     room
        LDI     r6, 0
room:   MOV16   r1, SHARED
        LD      r2, r1, 1               ; TAIL
        SUB     r2, r2, r4
        SUBI    r2, 1
        ANDI    r2, MASK                ; r2 = free entries
        TEST    r7, r7
        BZ      one
        SUBI    r2, 1                   ; one for the shift; C = 0: none free
        BNC     stop
one:    SUBI    r2, 1                   ; one for the character
        BNC     stop                    ; no room: the character is dropped whole
        MOV     r5, r0                  ; r2 = free entries after the write

stop:   WAITD   OVS                     ; the middle of the stop bit
        JP      RX, 0, ferr             ; low: framing error, the byte is dropped
        TEST    r5, r5
        BZ      rx                      ; dropped, or no room
        TEST    r7, r7
        BZ      put
        ADD     r0, r1, r4
        ST      r7, r0, RING - SHARED   ; the shift
        ADDI    r4, 1
        ANDI    r4, MASK
put:    ADD     r0, r1, r4
        ST      r5, r0, RING - SHARED   ; the character
        ADDI    r4, 1
        ANDI    r4, MASK
        ST      r4, r1, 0               ; publish HEAD, after the entries
        MOV     r3, r6                  ; the far end's case from now on
        CMPI    r2, LOW                 ; C: LOW or more still free
        BC      rx
        SETP    CTS_N, 1                ; nearly full: stop the sender
        JMP     rx

ferr:   WAITP   RX, 1                   ; wait for the line to return to idle
        JMP     rx

; ======================================== thread 1: the ring to the teleprinter
; r0 the code being sent, r1 the elements left, r2 HEAD as last read, r3
; scratch, r4 TAIL, r5 SHARED.
        .thread 1
        .tick   16                      ; the shortest tick the pairs are proved for
t1:     SETP    TX, 1                   ; idle: stop polarity (condition Z)
        MOV16   r5, SHARED
        LDI     r4, 0                   ; TAIL

idle1:  LD      r2, r5, 0               ; HEAD: poll until the ring holds a code
        CMP     r2, r4
        BZ      idle1
        ADD     r3, r5, r4
        LD      r0, r3, RING - SHARED   ; the code
        ADDI    r4, 1
        ANDI    r4, MASK
        ST      r4, r5, 1               ; publish TAIL, after the read
        SETD    2                       ; re-anchor: TD = two ticks on, so the
        SETP    TX, 0, D                ; latch is staged before NOW reaches TD
        WAITD   0                       ; and the start lands on that tick (rule 1)

frame1: SUB     r3, r4, r2              ; free entries, as of the HEAD read
        SUBI    r3, 1
        ANDI    r3, MASK
        CMPI    r3, HIGH                ; C: HIGH or more free
        BNC     nogo
        SETP    CTS_N, 0                ; let the sender go again
nogo:   LDI     r1, 5                   ; five code elements, element 1 first
bit1:   SHRI    r0, 1                   ; C = the next element
        BC      one1
        SETP    TX, 0, D                ; condition A
        JMP     next1
one1:   SETP    TX, 1, D                ; condition Z
next1:  WAITD   TPB                     ; the previous element ends, this one lands
        DJNZ    r1, bit1

        SETP    TX, 1, D                ; the stop element
        WAITD   TPB                     ; element 5 ends; the stop lands
        WAITD   STOP - LEAD             ; LEAD ticks before the stop ends
        LD      r2, r5, 0               ; HEAD
        CMP     r2, r4
        BZ      empty1
        ADD     r3, r5, r4
        LD      r0, r3, RING - SHARED   ; the next code
        ADDI    r4, 1
        ANDI    r4, MASK
        ST      r4, r5, 1               ; publish TAIL
        SETP    TX, 0, D                ; the next start element lands
        WAITD   LEAD                    ; exactly where the stop ends
        JMP     frame1

empty1: WAITD   LEAD                    ; finish the stop element, then poll
        JMP     idle1

; ====================================================== data, at load time
        .thread 0
        .org    SHARED
        .word   0                       ; HEAD
        .word   0                       ; TAIL

; The translation table (header): ITA2 combinations from S.1 Table 1,
; code element 1 in bit 0, condition Z = 1 (3.2).
        .equ    C_A = 0x03
        .equ    C_B = 0x19
        .equ    C_C = 0x0E
        .equ    C_D = 0x09
        .equ    C_E = 0x01
        .equ    C_F = 0x0D
        .equ    C_G = 0x1A
        .equ    C_H = 0x14
        .equ    C_I = 0x06
        .equ    C_J = 0x0B
        .equ    C_K = 0x0F
        .equ    C_L = 0x12
        .equ    C_M = 0x1C
        .equ    C_N = 0x0C
        .equ    C_O = 0x18
        .equ    C_P = 0x16
        .equ    C_Q = 0x17
        .equ    C_R = 0x0A
        .equ    C_S = 0x05
        .equ    C_T = 0x10
        .equ    C_U = 0x07
        .equ    C_V = 0x1E
        .equ    C_W = 0x13
        .equ    C_X = 0x1D
        .equ    C_Y = 0x15
        .equ    C_Z = 0x11
        .org    TABLE
        .word   0                       ; 0x00: none
        .word   0                       ; 0x01: none
        .word   0                       ; 0x02: none
        .word   0                       ; 0x03: none
        .word   0                       ; 0x04: none
        .word   0                       ; 0x05: none
        .word   0                       ; 0x06: none
        .word   FIG << 5 | C_J          ; 0x07 BEL
        .word   0                       ; 0x08: none
        .word   0                       ; 0x09: none
        .word   EITHER << 5 | 0x02      ; 0x0A LF
        .word   0                       ; 0x0B: none
        .word   0                       ; 0x0C: none
        .word   EITHER << 5 | 0x08      ; 0x0D CR
        .word   0                       ; 0x0E: none
        .word   0                       ; 0x0F: none
        .word   0                       ; 0x10: none
        .word   0                       ; 0x11: none
        .word   0                       ; 0x12: none
        .word   0                       ; 0x13: none
        .word   0                       ; 0x14: none
        .word   0                       ; 0x15: none
        .word   0                       ; 0x16: none
        .word   0                       ; 0x17: none
        .word   0                       ; 0x18: none
        .word   0                       ; 0x19: none
        .word   0                       ; 0x1A: none
        .word   0                       ; 0x1B: none
        .word   0                       ; 0x1C: none
        .word   0                       ; 0x1D: none
        .word   0                       ; 0x1E: none
        .word   0                       ; 0x1F: none
        .word   EITHER << 5 | SPACE     ; 0x20 space
        .word   0                       ; 0x21 !: none
        .word   0                       ; 0x22 ": none
        .word   0                       ; 0x23 #: none
        .word   0                       ; 0x24 $: none
        .word   0                       ; 0x25 %: none
        .word   0                       ; 0x26 &: none
        .word   FIG << 5 | C_S          ; 0x27 '
        .word   FIG << 5 | C_K          ; 0x28 (
        .word   FIG << 5 | C_L          ; 0x29 )
        .word   0                       ; 0x2A *: none
        .word   FIG << 5 | C_Z          ; 0x2B +
        .word   FIG << 5 | C_N          ; 0x2C ,
        .word   FIG << 5 | C_A          ; 0x2D -
        .word   FIG << 5 | C_M          ; 0x2E .
        .word   FIG << 5 | C_X          ; 0x2F /
        .word   FIG << 5 | C_P          ; 0x30 0
        .word   FIG << 5 | C_Q          ; 0x31 1
        .word   FIG << 5 | C_W          ; 0x32 2
        .word   FIG << 5 | C_E          ; 0x33 3
        .word   FIG << 5 | C_R          ; 0x34 4
        .word   FIG << 5 | C_T          ; 0x35 5
        .word   FIG << 5 | C_Y          ; 0x36 6
        .word   FIG << 5 | C_U          ; 0x37 7
        .word   FIG << 5 | C_I          ; 0x38 8
        .word   FIG << 5 | C_O          ; 0x39 9
        .word   FIG << 5 | C_C          ; 0x3A :
        .word   0                       ; 0x3B ;: none
        .word   0                       ; 0x3C <: none
        .word   FIG << 5 | C_V          ; 0x3D =
        .word   0                       ; 0x3E >: none
        .word   FIG << 5 | C_B          ; 0x3F ?
        .word   0                       ; 0x40 @: none
        .word   LET << 5 | C_A          ; 0x41 A
        .word   LET << 5 | C_B          ; 0x42 B
        .word   LET << 5 | C_C          ; 0x43 C
        .word   LET << 5 | C_D          ; 0x44 D
        .word   LET << 5 | C_E          ; 0x45 E
        .word   LET << 5 | C_F          ; 0x46 F
        .word   LET << 5 | C_G          ; 0x47 G
        .word   LET << 5 | C_H          ; 0x48 H
        .word   LET << 5 | C_I          ; 0x49 I
        .word   LET << 5 | C_J          ; 0x4A J
        .word   LET << 5 | C_K          ; 0x4B K
        .word   LET << 5 | C_L          ; 0x4C L
        .word   LET << 5 | C_M          ; 0x4D M
        .word   LET << 5 | C_N          ; 0x4E N
        .word   LET << 5 | C_O          ; 0x4F O
        .word   LET << 5 | C_P          ; 0x50 P
        .word   LET << 5 | C_Q          ; 0x51 Q
        .word   LET << 5 | C_R          ; 0x52 R
        .word   LET << 5 | C_S          ; 0x53 S
        .word   LET << 5 | C_T          ; 0x54 T
        .word   LET << 5 | C_U          ; 0x55 U
        .word   LET << 5 | C_V          ; 0x56 V
        .word   LET << 5 | C_W          ; 0x57 W
        .word   LET << 5 | C_X          ; 0x58 X
        .word   LET << 5 | C_Y          ; 0x59 Y
        .word   LET << 5 | C_Z          ; 0x5A Z
        .word   0                       ; 0x5B [: none
        .word   0                       ; 0x5C \: none
        .word   0                       ; 0x5D ]: none
        .word   0                       ; 0x5E ^: none
        .word   0                       ; 0x5F _: none
        .word   0                       ; 0x60 `: none
        .word   LET << 5 | C_A          ; 0x61 a
        .word   LET << 5 | C_B          ; 0x62 b
        .word   LET << 5 | C_C          ; 0x63 c
        .word   LET << 5 | C_D          ; 0x64 d
        .word   LET << 5 | C_E          ; 0x65 e
        .word   LET << 5 | C_F          ; 0x66 f
        .word   LET << 5 | C_G          ; 0x67 g
        .word   LET << 5 | C_H          ; 0x68 h
        .word   LET << 5 | C_I          ; 0x69 i
        .word   LET << 5 | C_J          ; 0x6A j
        .word   LET << 5 | C_K          ; 0x6B k
        .word   LET << 5 | C_L          ; 0x6C l
        .word   LET << 5 | C_M          ; 0x6D m
        .word   LET << 5 | C_N          ; 0x6E n
        .word   LET << 5 | C_O          ; 0x6F o
        .word   LET << 5 | C_P          ; 0x70 p
        .word   LET << 5 | C_Q          ; 0x71 q
        .word   LET << 5 | C_R          ; 0x72 r
        .word   LET << 5 | C_S          ; 0x73 s
        .word   LET << 5 | C_T          ; 0x74 t
        .word   LET << 5 | C_U          ; 0x75 u
        .word   LET << 5 | C_V          ; 0x76 v
        .word   LET << 5 | C_W          ; 0x77 w
        .word   LET << 5 | C_X          ; 0x78 x
        .word   LET << 5 | C_Y          ; 0x79 y
        .word   LET << 5 | C_Z          ; 0x7A z
        .word   0                       ; 0x7B {: none
        .word   0                       ; 0x7C |: none
        .word   0                       ; 0x7D }: none
        .word   0                       ; 0x7E ~: none
        .word   0                       ; 0x7F: none
